ior Antonio who enters with Pedro after Leonato and his brother step aside. Ursula identifies him by his nod and dry hand. Distinct stage role from the elder brother, whom other editions sometimes call Antonio.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.antonio_ursulas_dance_partner.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"f30f118d31eb546259264a15036514bb1077f09d6c71e3605a6ac30535671612":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-merchant-of-venice.r1"}],"etymology":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","exampleIds":[],"headword":"antoniyo","id":"shakespeare014.lex.antonio_venice_merchant","ipa":"","label":"antonio_venice_merchant","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"proper","pronunciation":"antoniyo","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-merchant-of-venice.r1"}],"definition":"The Venetian merchant and Bassanio's friend, who enters the bond with Shylock.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.antonio_venice_merchant.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"e7fcbf12e2964a7c1be24740bbd8b17edd970d19d3fcc3b8643350ec70164129":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Author-permitted phonetic adaptation of a personal or place name.","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"etymology":"Author-permitted phonetic adaptation of a personal or place name.","exampleIds":[],"headword":"antonius","id":"shakespeare014.lex.antonius_variant","ipa":"","label":"antonius_variant","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"proper","pronunciation":"antonius","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"definition":"The Antonius name variant used by Demetrius for Antony; same referent as lex.antony, not a second man or an internal lookup suffix.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.antonius_variant.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"6395c0863ed54b6efb3740a1ad07778a79541faea63efebc82647182022840df":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Author-permitted phonetic adaptation of a personal or place name.","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"etymology":"Author-permitted phonetic adaptation of a personal or place name.","exampleIds":[],"headword":"antoni","id":"shakespeare014.lex.antony","ipa":"","label":"antony","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"proper","pronunciation":"antoni","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"definition":"Marcus Antonius, the Roman commander and Cleopatra\u2019s lover. Not Antonio in All\u2019s Well. Antonius is a source name variant of this same person.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.antony.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"d4849852fc188bc068e935de784eb19a806c2c5c5e6ddd6baf53bb9e25a6ca86":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation/sense: kjv_anvil","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.coriolanus.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation/sense: kjv_anvil","exampleIds":[],"headword":"zargl\u00c9z\u00cben","id":"shakespeare014.lex.anvil_support","ipa":"","label":"anvil_support","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"zargl\u00e4zhwen","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.coriolanus.r1"}],"definition":"The metalworking support figuratively identified with the opponent\u2019s body enduring sword blows. Reviewed native metal-hold; not an anatomical object.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.anvil_support.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"aa57acb46c33f87d26cc2cc0cef93c0bb5a137784641b91d598bb332dce9f936":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Reviewed contextual sense of kjv_ape","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.as-you-lik