","label":"emilia_hermione_attendant","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"proper","pronunciation":"emilya","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-winters-tale.r1"}],"definition":"Hermione's attendant who tells Paulina of the baby's birth in confinement. A different person from Hippolyta's sister.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emilia_hermione_attendant.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"d2d33ba9fc2f91f63e5a2b4d56184ca7ced2446d6e54e1a5787417dd6b9b0a6d":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-two-noble-kinsmen.r1"}],"etymology":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","exampleIds":[],"headword":"emilya","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emilia_hippolyta_sister","ipa":"","label":"emilia_hippolyta_sister","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"proper","pronunciation":"emilya","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-two-noble-kinsmen.r1"}],"definition":"Hippolyta's sister. Palamon and Arcite see her from prison.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emilia_hippolyta_sister.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"bbea8b5a5e45814174b9996116581721644ad83258b3d060289d93a9d574afa7":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.othello.r1"}],"etymology":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","exampleIds":[],"headword":"emiliya","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emilia_iago_wife","ipa":"","label":"emilia_iago_wife","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"proper","pronunciation":"emiliya","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.othello.r1"}],"definition":"Iago's wife and Desdemona's attendant.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emilia_iago_wife.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"329b853e4bfe00abb14076e0213d46c3718a8795bd576b9ec7520b10a66e2045":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-two-noble-kinsmen.r1"}],"etymology":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","exampleIds":[],"headword":"emili","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emily_theseus_address","ipa":"","label":"emily_theseus_address","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"proper","pronunciation":"emili","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-two-noble-kinsmen.r1"}],"definition":"Theseus's shorter form when addressing Emilia after he orders Arcite to attend her. Both forms denote Hippolyta's sister.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emily_theseus_address.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"20ed415a991d5bbc64295320a0b71a74c1b39fb51fa71ee6abfe396cffb6b3ac":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.henry-vi-part-2.r1"}],"etymology":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","exampleIds":[],"headword":"emanuel","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emmanuel_clerk","ipa":"","label":"emmanuel_clerk","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"proper","pronunciation":"emanuel","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.henry-vi-part-2.r1"}],"definition":"The given name of the clerk of Chartham, which Dick recognizes as a name used at the head of letters.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emmanuel_clerk.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"5b7ca3c5120a451c88d59bfc45c4014319cc2de66e085567bfd3bc4b296e1eff":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: emperor_roman_throne + royal","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.titus-andronicus.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: emperor_roman_throne + royal","exampleIds":[],"headword":"var\u00dfnvar\u00dfnvar\u00dfnna","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emperal_countryman_error","ipa":"","label":"emperal_countryman_error","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"var\u014dnvar\u014dnvar\u014dnna","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.titus-andronicus.r1"}],"definition":"The countryman\u2019s mixed form for emperor. His native form combines the imperial title with royal.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emperal_countryman_error.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"5abc100ad0bc0a8d8130632472e4b8e147fe47872bff50326e6228233e9907c4":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Reviewed contextual sense/identity of commander","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"etymology":"Reviewed contextual sense/identity of commander","exampleIds":[],"headword":"nor\u00d3as\u00dflum","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emperor_commander","ipa":"","label":"emperor_commander","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"northas\u014dlum","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"definition":"Enobarbus\u2019s emperor address to Antony as his supreme military commander, the Roman imperator sense. Native command-person; not a claim that Antony already holds a later sole imperial office or a borrowed ordinary title.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emperor_commander.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"0bc38bca41d0246aac0501a9ce8efe40fa025ed51b3565916f5bfc7748e542c7":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Reviewed contextual sense/identity of emperor_commander","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"etymology":"Reviewed contextual sense/identity of emperor_commander","exampleIds":[],"headword":"nor\u00d3as\u00dflum","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emperor_dream","ipa":"","label":"emperor_dream","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"northas\u014dlum","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"definition":"The supreme Antony of Cleopatra\u2019s dream, expanded from her actual commander to the imagined world-striding ruler. No claim that the historical Antony held a later sole imperial office.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emperor_dream.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"cc235a051ed970e9c6b41a170c782903556f8ea9c8c018aa8e8fa73e8620063b":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: king + king","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-winters-tale.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: king + king","exampleIds":[],"headword":"var\u00dfnvar\u00dfn","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emperor_dynastic_ruler","ipa":"","label":"emperor_dynastic_ruler","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"var\u014dnvar\u014dn","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-winters-tale.r1"}],"definition":"The dynastic sovereign of Russia whom Hermione names as her father. Separate from Roman imperator as a military address.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emperor_dynastic_ruler.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"9fd0aee259f6430d32c4855698597b8bf219ae739c19aade08ed28e111b3d43c":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: imperial + lord","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-two-gentlemen-of-verona.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: imperial + lord","exampleIds":[],"headword":"var\u00dfnjo\u00d9arvenek","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emperor_milan_court","ipa":"","label":"emperor_milan_court","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"var\u014dnjongarvenek","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-two-gentlemen-of-verona.r1"}],"definition":"Title for the ruler of the court to which the young gentlemen travel. The local cast list also designates him Duke.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emperor_milan_court.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"5a68c91ef25213dd459ae4b9a6116e07c2123264558551f257863f9c68f53bd3":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: emperor_dynastic_ruler","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.titus-andronicus.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: emperor_dynastic_ruler","exampleIds":[],"headword":"var\u00dfnvar\u00dfn","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emperor_roman_throne","ipa":"","label":"emperor_roman_throne","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"var\u014dnvar\u014dn","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.titus-andronicus.r1"}],"definition":"The Roman imperial office for which the brothers compete and which Saturninus receives.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emperor_roman_throne.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"0a6d9ac863da3e97ec2e7f4e8a8823e5e0990fcaeb52c96e3ffd1d0ca59eed8d":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation: word + force","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation: word + force","exampleIds":[],"headword":"tonjorgor","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emphasis_speech","ipa":"","label":"emphasis_speech","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"tonjorgor","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"definition":"Force or marked stress of an utterance, Charmian\u2019s emphatic praise of Caesar; not an added numeral or physical blow.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emphasis_speech.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"a4234bac07c36b791915fc3321283ef6a7d33e006c9b0e9c7aff21918ddac825":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation/sense: strong + say","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.coriolanus.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation/sense: strong + say","exampleIds":[],"headword":"jw\u00ebnenplestentonda","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emphasize_stress","ipa":"","label":"emphasize_stress","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"verb","pronunciation":"jw\u00fcnenplestentonda","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.coriolanus.r1"}],"definition":"Stress or press a point in argument, source enforce his pride. Native strong-say rather than physical enforcement of the emotion.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.emphasize_stress.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"ac8c00e9398392dc65736377d0c12f174df3edce2644d1a23630aab672debbff":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: empire + lord","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.king-john.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: empire + lord","exampleIds":[],"headword":"var\u00dfnjo\u00d9arvenek","id":"shakespeare014.lex.empire_sovereign","ipa":"","label":"empire_sovereign","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"var\u014dnjongarvenek","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.king-john.r1"}],"definition":"The sovereign to whom old Sir Robert is sent in Germany. The title denotes a ruling monarch here.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.empire_sovereign.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"51e5286d4b28d422250ab2b20cd97278baebf6ec1b1e2ac35daf9e1cf56d0793":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation/sense: guess + medicine","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.coriolanus.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation/sense: guess + medicine","exampleIds":[],"headword":"peksfadervoren","id":"shakespeare014.lex.empiricutic_remedy","ipa":"","label":"empiricutic_remedy","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"modifier","pronunciation":"peksfadervoren","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.coriolanus.r1"}],"definition":"Menenius dismisses even Galenic prescriptions as uncertain quack practice beside his joyful news. Deliberately pompous native guess-medicine; the character exaggerates, not a medical assertion or a literal translation of modern empirical science.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.empiricutic_remedy.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"916c85c33f7a62f3a34aa40f26bdfeb25e5f4efe29563a5954283c5c61dc9401":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: empress_sovereign","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.titus-andronicus.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: empress_sovereign","exampleIds":[],"headword":"var\u00dfnjo\u00d9var\u00dfna","id":"shakespeare014.lex.empress_roman_throne","ipa":"","label":"empress_roman_throne","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"var\u014dnjongvar\u014dna","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.titus-andronicus.r1"}],"definition":"The Roman imperial dignity proposed for Lavinia and then conferred on Tamora.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.empress_roman_throne.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"2a5aa0eb886a63f866a7dd2621957431034e8b1abfe11b7d1aa23cd50b7402a5":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation: empire + queen","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation: empire + queen","exampleIds":[],"headword":"var\u00dfnjo\u00d9var\u00dfna","id":"shakespeare014.lex.empress_sovereign","ipa":"","label":"empress_sovereign","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"var\u014dnjongvar\u014dna","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"definition":"Imperial female ruler as Iras\u2019s honorific address to Cleopatra. Native dominion-queen, not a phonetic ordinary title or an assertion of a new formal office.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.empress_sovereign.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"8efae44ba55f516dcb4c8a07fa08e581bfe14a3d725396a213ba5c3d0fa25458":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.titus-andronicus.r1"}],"etymology":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","exampleIds":[],"headword":"enkeladus","id":"shakespeare014.lex.enceladus_giant","ipa":"","label":"enceladus_giant","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"proper","pronunciation":"enkeladus","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.titus-andronicus.r1"}],"definition":"The giant named by Aaron as an imagined challenger to his defence of his infant son.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.enceladus_giant.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"e1df4db91223ac4cf13fc20c90a75361175c729c9bcbda14c263251d0f4009b1":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Reviewed contextual sense of kjv_enclose","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.lucrece.r1"}],"etymology":"Reviewed contextual sense of kjv_enclose","exampleIds":[],"headword":"\u00e7l\u00d7ono\u00c3t\u00e7","id":"shakespeare014.lex.enclose","ipa":"","label":"enclose","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"verb","pronunciation":"\u016bl\u0113onojzht\u016b","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.lucrece.r1"}],"definition":"Hold within a surrounding boundary, including the armies of complexion enclosing a gaze.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.enclose.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"f055b15bb086ce606fd7503df4befc8d9ba32c9de70beff6d369c5b50d73051f":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Reviewed contextual sense of end","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.shakespeares-sonnets.r1"}],"etymology":"Reviewed contextual sense of end","exampleIds":[],"headword":"h\u00eb\u00d5","id":"shakespeare014.lex.ending_added","ipa":"","label":"ending_added","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"h\u00fc\u0167h","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.shakespeares-sonnets.r1"}],"definition":"An appended completion of an utterance, the not-you ending in Sonnet 145; not a death.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.ending_added.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"49c4e50968fcd4902ef24976e7de14f1ed8208648c45e429f749b1eed3415265":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation/sense: endure","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.coriolanus.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation/sense: endure","exampleIds":[],"headword":"dlen\u00dbeminvenor","id":"shakespeare014.lex.endurance_capacity","ipa":"","label":"endurance_capacity","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"dlensheminvenor","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.coriolanus.r1"}],"definition":"Capacity or tolerance for bearing something, source noble sufferance. Explicit nominal endure; neither a command to suffer nor a claim that the nobles must approve.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.endurance_capacity.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"bf1465d9fedfd571778c36321a1ed377eb248e645a15c412f22caa7b65347015":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","sourceId":"shakespeare014.sour