n + garment. Distinct from courage, and no unstated specific item or price.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.finery_clothing.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"acf8dd0b960eee01c2f4121869ac5d59d9dfe7764a7c6e24c46e685365a1e389":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Reviewed contextual sense/identity of kjv_fingernail","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"etymology":"Reviewed contextual sense/identity of kjv_fingernail","exampleIds":[],"headword":"jeljeran","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fingernail_body","ipa":"","label":"fingernail_body","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"jeljeran","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"definition":"Octavia\u2019s prepared nails in Antony\u2019s threatened humiliation of Cleopatra; bodily nails, not fastening spikes.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fingernail_body.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"82b41bf07d41183c9e27a6281f200068bc6446b2b1369e38f432bd7de2ae3f20":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Author-permitted geographic-name adaptation","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.henry-iv-part-1.r1"}],"etymology":"Author-permitted geographic-name adaptation","exampleIds":[],"headword":"finzburi","id":"shakespeare014.lex.finsbury_fields","ipa":"","label":"finsbury_fields","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"proper","pronunciation":"finzburi","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.henry-iv-part-1.r1"}],"definition":"A London locality in Hotspur's taunt about Kate's gentle oaths.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.finsbury_fields.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"bbf5b065d4c99ad5608aea2ddca490d1fe91d1f8a6a59e391354db84f280fe28":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: iron + strike","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.henry-v.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: iron + strike","exampleIds":[],"headword":"fer\u00ebnsleb","id":"shakespeare014.lex.firk_threat","ipa":"","label":"firk_threat","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"verb","pronunciation":"fer\u00fcnsleb","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.henry-v.r1"}],"definition":"Pistol's beating threat, with the iron sound picked up from Fer.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.firk_threat.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"e4cf4549d79a570f422a63686d95179b19028a6331818df6595b523d3d9c48c8":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Reviewed contextual sense of strong","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.shakespeares-sonnets.r1"}],"etymology":"Reviewed contextual sense of strong","exampleIds":[],"headword":"jw\u00ebnenplesten","id":"shakespeare014.lex.firm_ground","ipa":"","label":"firm_ground","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"modifier","pronunciation":"jw\u00fcnenplesten","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.shakespeares-sonnets.r1"}],"definition":"Firmness of solid ground contrasted with the sea in Sonnet 64; physical resistance, not a moral promise or commercial company.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.firm_ground.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"aa6b8982cfac4a769ae26cade07d4d2f0b9c84df58b9a5f19d047795e2f34ff8":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Reviewed contextual sense of fish","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.venus-and-adonis.r1"}],"etymology":"Reviewed contextual sense of fish","exampleIds":[],"headword":"drig\u00e1l","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fish_creature","ipa":"","label":"fish_creature","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"drig\u00f4l","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.venus-and-adonis.r1"}],"definition":"The aquatic creature, noun sense of the author-attested fish root; not the action of fishing.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fish_creature.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"7278e9f5605a5bc6ac7e76b99e005f21c88295f8da6abdc7a00ea397b4dd8663":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: water + fence","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-tempest.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: water + fence","exampleIds":[],"headword":"yems\u00d7thelir","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fish_dam_weir","ipa":"","label":"fish_dam_weir","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"yems\u0113thelir","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-tempest.r1"}],"definition":"A barrier that traps fish, which Caliban says he will no longer build for Prospero.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fish_dam_weir.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"518c89d88271cf10c7e4c645be5d1b76727f8103de8669e9ea24d6bd35a143cd":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation: fish_creature + wing","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation: fish_creature + wing","exampleIds":[],"headword":"drig\u00e1lv\u00ddlawin","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fish_fin","ipa":"","label":"fish_fin","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"drig\u00f4lv\u012blawin","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"definition":"A fish\u2019s projecting swimming organ, fish-wing by analogy. No claim that it flies or a distinct fish species is supplied.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fish_fin.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"260b72097145747aef7a6c44ea8a62db762afed24844388836459714a8e3b1eb":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: fish + person","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.pericles.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: fish + person","exampleIds":[],"headword":"drig\u00e1ls\u00dflum","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fisherman_net_worker","ipa":"","label":"fisherman_net_worker","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"drig\u00f4ls\u014dlum","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.pericles.r1"}],"definition":"A person fishing for a living. The three fishermen have separate ordinal speech labels.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fisherman_net_worker.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"e25f3429e3062701fe4b99df3a4519551ae5c020380c11fe24f264512f57a81b":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Reviewed contextual sense/identity of kjv_fishing_hook","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"etymology":"Reviewed contextual sense/identity of kjv_fishing_hook","exampleIds":[],"headword":"drig\u00e1llunor gl\u00c9z\u00cben","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fishing_hook","ipa":"","label":"fishing_hook","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"drig\u00f4llunor gl\u00e4zhwen","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"definition":"The curved hook piercing fish jaws in Cleopatra\u2019s fishing fantasy. Native fish-hook; no Biblical judgment, geometric angle or foreign hook loan is imported.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fishing_hook.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"d372f39c44ded1bd889296d8b78bf16169c2a3851facd0028de21cfa5df2255a":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: fish_creature + merchant","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.hamlet.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: fish_creature + merchant","exampleIds":[],"headword":"drig\u00e1lkamiz\u00e3\u00d1ts\u00dflum","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fishmonger_trader","ipa":"","label":"fishmonger_trader","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"drig\u00f4lkamizoichts\u014dlum","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.hamlet.r1"}],"definition":"A seller of fish. Hamlet calls Polonius this before speaking of his daughter; the trade name keeps the insult available to the listener.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fishmonger_trader.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"58f1c5d9e3dabd13d70c2567398a9789e4ae70a588d40d5934092501df3885cd":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: return + disease_ailment","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-two-noble-kinsmen.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: return + disease_ailment","exampleIds":[],"headword":"sezif\u00c7\u00c3muren","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fit_recurring_bout","ipa":"","label":"fit_recurring_bout","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"sezif\u00e3jzhmuren","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-two-noble-kinsmen.r1"}],"definition":"The bout that the Daughter expects in the man she takes for Palamon. She specifies an hour without describing any symptoms.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fit_recurring_bout.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"5843829e17d57fbd44500ed4c84f2e48512e72a61ff9dd96c13d2775c07aae65":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: stink + weasel","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.othello.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: stink + weasel","exampleIds":[],"headword":"murmayatr\u00e3b\u00d3\u00ddrjil","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fitchew_polecat","ipa":"","label":"fitchew_polecat","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"murmayatroibth\u012brjil","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.othello.r1"}],"definition":"The strong-smelling mustelid Cassio calls Bianca as she approaches. Perfuming the animal caps his insult.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fitchew_polecat.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"4fa7b28d1d86fc7320864ae0c7c0e760eb0496ddedb37b2ad45506bff97eae63":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation: fitting + manner","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation: fitting + manner","exampleIds":[],"headword":"memilf\u00c7l","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fitting_order","ipa":"","label":"fitting_order","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"memilf\u00e3l","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"definition":"Fitting correspondence or proper order, Iras\u2019s sarcastic demand that Isis match an ugly rogue with cuckoldry.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fitting_order.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"5b6c25b03cac60d2ac5e8af2e4028e5b5584a250dbd9a2f6c51b4296aed0399a":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.richard-ii.r1"}],"etymology":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","exampleIds":[],"headword":"fitswater","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fitzwater_richard_appellant","ipa":"","label":"fitzwater_richard_appellant","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"proper","pronunciation":"fitswater","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.richard-ii.r1"}],"definition":"The lord who challenges Aumerle and reports two prisoners' heads sent from Oxford.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fitzwater_richard_appellant.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"82a460d3bf6f5635aff70bdaf8f195b3c78f5fca7306983f5ea11c6489d2f3f6":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: jaw + swelling_body","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-taming-of-the-shrew.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: jaw + swelling_body","exampleIds":[],"headword":"daz\u00ebmniragum","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fives_horse","ipa":"","label":"fives_horse","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"daz\u00fcmniragum","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-taming-of-the-shrew.r1"}],"definition":"The fives or strangles affecting glands near the horse's jaws. The name carries no numerical assertion.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.fives_horse.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"7b7e59ef4c3455cbbed04923b6141f9776c3852c1d2b61ae1ac5cd4fe971931d":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation: water + reed","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation: water + reed","exampleIds":[],"headword":"yems\u00d7v\u00eblin","id":"shakespeare014.lex.flag_waterplant","ipa":"","label":"flag_waterplant","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"yems\u0113v\u00fclin","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"definition":"A reed-like water plant drifting with the stream; the flag of Caesar\u2019s common-body simile is not a banner. No exact modern species is asserted.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.flag_waterplant.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"9fb2130da2590d6c670233ddc3cf027cf9a0f01656e421b3e2c9e1aa5f0ce35c":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation: commander + ship","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation: commander + sh