d portion, Cleopatra\u2019s self-image as a morsel for a monarch; not an exact measure or a whole meal.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.morsel_food.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"7fc82e6577f9c3e97fa6ff622596c2c43508426288d798b0c873f9c7c3dc3014":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: swallow","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-winters-tale.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: swallow","exampleIds":[],"headword":"niwem","id":"shakespeare014.lex.morsel_swallow_game","ipa":"","label":"morsel_swallow_game","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"verb","pronunciation":"niwem","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-winters-tale.r1"}],"definition":"Gulp down as in the flapdragon game. The Shepherd's Son imagines the sea playing the game with the ship.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.morsel_swallow_game.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"fb6480a0361d38c6cb28891516e4617463d7a2ff163c16257ac7b1789daa0d3b":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Reviewed contextual sense of deadly","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.as-you-like-it.r1"}],"etymology":"Reviewed contextual sense of deadly","exampleIds":[],"headword":"n\u00d7nenna","id":"shakespeare014.lex.mortal_intensive","ipa":"","label":"mortal_intensive","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"degree","pronunciation":"n\u0113nenna","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.as-you-like-it.r1"}],"definition":"Context-bound death-root intensification in Touchstone\u2019s mortal in folly: excessive rather than a claim that every lover dies. Retains the death-family echo of ordinary mortal in the preceding clause; an authored literary extension, not a universal rule changing the ordinary mortality adjective.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.mortal_intensive.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"49659566dd91532e9c9a069f361afdf8d2896320e92151855fbe53735d81d7c7":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: life + stir","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.hamlet.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: life + stir","exampleIds":[],"headword":"\u00d5\u00dfrda\u00c3","id":"shakespeare014.lex.mortal_tumult","ipa":"","label":"mortal_tumult","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"\u0167h\u014drdajzh","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.hamlet.r1"}],"definition":"The trouble and commotion of mortal life that a sleeper might cast off. Retains the disturbance in the source's coil.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.mortal_tumult.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"e0fd13223dfd24b4e31aae7a88e1b06b585867d4e6c7e4df0100fb5b17624746":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation: lime + bind","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation: lime + bind","exampleIds":[],"headword":"lim\u00ebnkl\u00e1rjem","id":"shakespeare014.lex.mortar_binder","ipa":"","label":"mortar_binder","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"lim\u00fcnkl\u00f4rjem","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"definition":"A building binder or mortar in the marriage-as-fortress image. Native lime-bind is a lexicalized material category, not a modern cement recipe or a new physical building project.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.mortar_binder.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"e136d24212656f5339f27ffa50dced69a3dabaac2dcff181f422c55e8d261d2e":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native lexical derivation: debt + pledge","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.shakespeares-sonnets.r1"}],"etymology":