 Games. Distinct from Mount Olympus.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.olympia_games_site.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"ce28ce3e639c35a04b65dc021ccb6d26c1e05bf6cfc9b95cea330b46ccc3fa3e":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.coriolanus.r1"}],"etymology":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","exampleIds":[],"headword":"olimpus","id":"shakespeare014.lex.olympus_mountain","ipa":"","label":"olympus_mountain","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"proper","pronunciation":"olimpus","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.coriolanus.r1"}],"definition":"The named mountain imagined inclining in supplication before a molehill.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.olympus_mountain.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"192d8d8413138266ebbc629c26dde3738c6ae0f5d914b297c3fc6e626678762a":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Reviewed contextual sense of omit","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.as-you-like-it.r1"}],"etymology":"Reviewed contextual sense of omit","exampleIds":[],"headword":"vutthar","id":"shakespeare014.lex.omission_delay","ipa":"","label":"omission_delay","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"vutthar","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.as-you-like-it.r1"}],"definition":"Omission or failure to answer immediately, the source omittance. Nominal use of native leave-out; distinguished from acquittal or repayment.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.omission_delay.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"a3dfc3876e7b8cb476539a14afd40088d881516babd0c41e59585d2b00642f6f":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: change + light + gem","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.twelfth-night.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: change + light + gem","exampleIds":[],"headword":"\u00e3lumb\u00d7\u00c7ryem","id":"shakespeare014.lex.opal_changing_gem","ipa":"","label":"opal_changing_gem","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"oilumb\u0113\u00e3ryem","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.twelfth-night.r1"}],"definition":"Gem whose changing color figures Orsino's changing mind.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.opal_changing_gem.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"d7ce19515f107cad14af5d0fd80b447faf85fd2e774fcad8f3f8514f181a5a67":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Native lexical derivation from lock, open","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.venus-and-adonis.r1"}],"etymology":"Native lexical derivation from lock, open","exampleIds":[],"headword":"zarel\u00c5el","id":"shakespeare014.lex.open_by_pick","ipa":"","label":"open_by_pick","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"verb","pronunciation":"zarel\u0101el","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.venus-and-adonis.r1"}],"definition":"Open a lock by manipulating its fastening rather than using its key; no method is specified.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.open_by_pick.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"3a4d61554a9e1437a5d0161e8c4f1cbb2aaf01b5970d9facd22827ea61a51517":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.hamlet.r1"}],"etymology":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","exampleIds":[],"headword":"ofelia","id":"shakespeare014.lex.ophelia_daughter","ipa":"","label":"ophelia_daughter","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"proper","pronunciation":"ofelia","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.hamlet.r1"}],"definition":"Polonius's daughter and Laertes's sister. She answers her brother's warning before her father questions her about Hamlet.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.ophelia_daughter.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"ec9b87235a6828c5a6d1a299dec258d1b329b6d1a4f5cd00057f919487268722":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Reviewed contextual sense of opportunity","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.lucrece.r1"}],"etymology":"Reviewed contextual sense of opportunity","exampleIds":[],"headword":"venor","id":"shakespeare014.lex.opportunity_personified","ipa":"","label":"opportunity_personified","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"proper","pronunciation":"venor","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.lucrece.r1"}],"definition":"Opportunity personified as enabling good or evil acts; the speech accuses its bias toward evil.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.opportunity_personified.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"9f5a01129715ca7a0938c1eed126e40ab21f213aafb4b583a2505d2ae8c0b091":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation/sense: orange + fruit","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.coriolanus.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation/sense: orange + fruit","exampleIds":[],"headword":"\u00ebnavenam","id":"shakespeare014.lex.orange_fruit","ipa":"","label":"orange_fruit","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"\u00fcnavenam","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.coriolanus.r1"}],"definition":"The citrus fruit sold by the orange-wife, lexicalized native colour-fruit category. Distinct from the orange colour adjective and not every orange-coloured fruit.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.orange_fruit.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"f85eba19bf738579ed2fca3dc0e686bb32b427402d3e1c30bc769fa3f0a961ff":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation 