sequently a speaker.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.rambures_lord.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"067b28876bae151920bbacecd58efc1436a96e746f1d9c070e2f7a5badad6a42":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.richard-ii.r1"}],"etymology":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","exampleIds":[],"headword":"ramston","id":"shakespeare014.lex.ramston_returning_knight","ipa":"","label":"ramston_returning_knight","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"proper","pronunciation":"ramston","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.richard-ii.r1"}],"definition":"Sir John Ramston, one of the knights returning with Hereford.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.ramston_returning_knight.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"3afbbfe064bc4dfabdaf36fa8259abc3c9d63ace01b3b8eb1a857a3a93b45f49":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Reviewed contextual sense/identity of arrange","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"etymology":"Reviewed contextual sense/identity of arrange","exampleIds":[],"headword":"hif\u00e5ror","id":"shakespeare014.lex.ranged_ordered","ipa":"","label":"ranged_ordered","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"modifier","pronunciation":"hifouror","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"definition":"Ordered or disposed in ranks, the empire imagined as an encompassing arch. The exact architectural geography is not supplied.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.ranged_ordered.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"6a9b0d985b02aee963bd22fc502a5e041586808afb25f4a609b292454d97f48d":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Reviewed contextual sense of grow","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.as-you-like-it.r1"}],"etymology":"Reviewed contextual sense of grow","exampleIds":[],"headword":"ragum","id":"shakespeare014.lex.rank_excessive","ipa":"","label":"rank_excessive","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"modifier","pronunciation":"ragum","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.as-you-like-it.r1"}],"definition":"Overgrown or excessive, with an unfavourable implication in honesty ranker than wit. Native growth modifier preserves the exaggerated comparison without substituting social rank or committing solely to rotten smell.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.rank_excessive.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"8564c0068f743b6ac572d7ed1b1a1561064824ea7bcf91be42e088e5c1def07d":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Reviewed contextual sense of grow","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.as-you-like-it.r1"}],"etymology":"Reviewed contextual sense of grow","exampleIds":[],"headword":"ragum","id":"shakespeare014.lex.rank_growth_rebellion","ipa":"","label":"rank_growth_rebellion","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"ragum","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.as-you-like-it.r1"}],"definition":"Nominal growth, excessive growth metaphorically applied to Orlando\u2019s rebellion by Oliver; a growth he proposes to dose with medicine, not an offensive smell or official rank.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.rank_growth_rebellion.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"85f9376699e2f755168cda956f3f6e98ad6ae746a132263ec2b96650e5d541b7":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Reviewed contextual sense of foul","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.shakespeares-sonnets.r1"}],"etymology":"Reviewed contextual sense of foul","exampleIds":[],"headword":"murmayat","id":"shakespeare014.lex.rank_odour","ipa":"","label":"rank_odour","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"modifier","pronunciation":"murmayat","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.shakespeares-sonnets.r1"}],"definition":"Offensively strong smell attributed by observers in Sonnet 69; distinct from social rank.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.rank_odour.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"4e04611c24a137e7cf2999f014beb440e4199b2f05819a21690a3838b5f898f8":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Reviewed contextual sense of file","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.as-you-like-it.r1"}],"etymology":"Reviewed contextual sense of file","exampleIds":[],"headword":"nor\u00d3ajer","id":"shakespeare014.lex.rank_procession","ipa":"","label":"rank_procession","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"northajer","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.as-you-like-it.r1"}],"definition":"An ordered row or procession, the supplied butter-women\u2019s rank going to market. Retains rank rather than adopting an editor\u2019s rack or rate emendation; its comparison to monotonous verse is contextual, not a social rank.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.rank_procession.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"cd63e07cd1edc5ee43f02a959cb72145a01f13b0c442a6746e4b225a76e76488":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Author-permitted phonetic adaptation of a personal or place name.","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"etymology":"Author-permitted phonetic adaptation of a personal or place name.","exampleIds":[],"headword":"ranius","id":"shakespeare014.lex.rannius","ipa":"","label":"rannius","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"proper","pronunciation":"ranius","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"definition":"Rannius, named in the supplied scene-two entrance.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.rannius.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"a6541e38901ebafa73f2eae91995d83246ad4ac094e17761b41b64c562304d2e":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation/sense: ransom","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.coriolanus.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation/sense: ransom","exampleIds":[],"headword":"maserv\u00df\u00d5","id":"shakespeare014.lex.ransom_release","ipa":"","label":"ransom_release","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"verb","pronunciation":"maserv\u014d\u0167h","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.coriolanus.r1"}],"definition":"Release a captive against ransom, Lartius\u2019s administration of the captured city; the fee and fate of individual captives remain unspecified.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.ransom_release.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"24471adb89f1bba9809c3793cc01188452052377aa7d7d917885c9b9f4da98ab":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: rape","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.titus-andronicus.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: rape","exampleIds":[],"headword":"jorgorz\u00ebra","id":"shakespeare014.lex.rape_chiron_disguise","ipa":"","label":"rape_chiron_disguise","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"proper","pronunciation":"jorgorz\u00fcra","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.titus-andronicus.r1"}],"definition":"Rape or Rapine, the personified role assumed by Chiron in the visit to Titus.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.rape_chiron_disguise.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"d653dc34c077931116b8d6f5bb3d8d8ff16f1aaf8095491a3d85cedfdede6e59":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: thrust + sword","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.hamlet.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: thrust + sword","exampleIds":[],"headword":"terastnerd