 distinct from Sir Rowland de Boys and the ballad figure.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.rowland_vienna_contact.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"ad60ac800038f68bfbd3e15db3533fa63eb4592ddc0550e535045ee2d727870d":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: king + journey","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.hamlet.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: king + journey","exampleIds":[],"headword":"var\u00dfnr\u00ddy\u00d3","id":"shakespeare014.lex.royal_progress","ipa":"","label":"royal_progress","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"var\u014dnr\u012byth","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.hamlet.r1"}],"definition":"A sovereign's ceremonial journey through a territory. Hamlet makes the food chain a route for a king to tour a beggar's guts.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.royal_progress.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"c9cd5ed0753fa199a18118fae48e201c6512ee13cf57b2e90fdeead01b1f0497":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native lexical derivation: king + staff","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.lucrece.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native lexical derivation: king + staff","exampleIds":[],"headword":"var\u00dfnteral","id":"shakespeare014.lex.royal_sceptre","ipa":"","label":"royal_sceptre","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"var\u014dnteral","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.lucrece.r1"}],"definition":"The royal staff, here imagined as striking a beggar; no phonetic foreign ordinary word.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.royal_sceptre.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"4461ab05addb9e70bd17821b4f49fe91541501809e462b7a542fc7a96aea76c7":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native lexical derivation: rule + people","sourceId":"shakespeare0