 derivation from breath, shut","exampleIds":[],"headword":"eni\u00d1\u00d7bra","id":"shakespeare014.lex.smother","ipa":"","label":"smother","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"verb","pronunciation":"enich\u0113bra","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.venus-and-adonis.r1"}],"definition":"Overwhelm or stifle breath; in the poem a hyperbolic threat to smother with kisses, not an added assertion of death.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.smother.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"6a3adea87014765000a43c366d5a35f710300c98a455f4c9093a20423222f4dc":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Reviewed contextual sense of smother","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.shakespeares-sonnets.r1"}],"etymology":"Reviewed contextual sense of smother","exampleIds":[],"headword":"eni\u00d1\u00d7bra","id":"shakespeare014.lex.smother_conceal","ipa":"","label":"smother_conceal","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"verb","pronunciation":"enich\u0113bra","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.shakespeares-sonnets.r1"}],"definition":"Stifle or cover from exposure, the mutual faults smothered by love in early Sonnet 138; no literal killing is added.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.smother_conceal.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"cee4b610f9631495f7363c5d4bdbdeeb623627885a6de3d8fc1e918799dd1577":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.king-lear.r1"}],"etymology":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","exampleIds":[],"headword":"smulkin","id":"shakespeare014.lex.smulkin_fiend","ipa":"","label":"smulkin_fiend","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"proper","pronunciation":"smulkin","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.king-lear.r1"}],"definition":"The fiend Poor Tom bids be quiet.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.smulkin_fiend.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"3757df0141bd7c837a39233168e12da13cac552a341f3e71aa83389cb6a9a11d":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation: bridle + bar","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation: bridle + bar","exampleIds":[],"headword":"thalzerzaril","id":"shakespeare014.lex.snaffle_bit","ipa":"","label":"snaffle_bit","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"thalzerzaril","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"definition":"The snaffle bridle mouthpiece in Antony\u2019s comparison of ruling a third of the world and controlling a wife. Native bridle-bar; no dimensions or exact mechanical joint are invented.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.snaffle_bit.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"067bc4053c0118bb2eaecd5c563118f6e820e033fcc71f3837aae871729626e8":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: snare","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.henry-iv-part-2.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: snare","exampleIds":[],"headword":"zerin","id":"shakespeare014.lex.snare_officer","ipa":"","label":"snare_officer","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"proper","pronunciation":"zerin","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.henry-iv-part-2.r1"}],"definition":"Fang's assistant. His telling name uses the native word for a trap.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.snare_officer.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"5aa4a71f2255db92d120d17b610c14944a3c25b364a173facbff5e31b8213cff":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Author-permitted phonetic personal-name adaptation","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.h