, the soothing tongue in early Sonnet 138; can carry flattery in context without asserting truth.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.soothe_gently.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"8a87949423caa114e58dde2fa492ed2c83f387d3a4fb8a22c583dbf401e02461":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Reviewed contextual sense/identity of kjv_soothsayer","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"etymology":"Reviewed contextual sense/identity of kjv_soothsayer","exampleIds":[],"headword":"jelamk\u00c5b\u00cffs\u00dflum","id":"shakespeare014.lex.soothsayer_role","ipa":"","label":"soothsayer_role","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"jelamk\u0101b\u00cffs\u014dlum","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"definition":"The unnamed diviner in Cleopatra\u2019s household. Keeps the inherited native divination-person role without importing Daniel\u2019s court or certifying the predictions.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.soothsayer_role.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"26ac0772563d1c7fcfbfb9aba6e2ea9dda9921c83a1a6225c6d8e783dd900a7d":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: kjv_persia + king","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-merchant-of-venice.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: kjv_persia + king","exampleIds":[],"headword":"persiavar\u00dfn","id":"shakespeare014.lex.sophy_persian_title","ipa":"","label":"sophy_persian_title","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"persiavar\u014dn","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-merchant-of-venice.r1"}],"definition":"The Persian ruler's title in Morocco's claim about his scimitar. A different Persian prince is mentioned immediately after.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.sophy_persian_title.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"4724161466ddc8e82f0c8df341e28f6167a9336281c1198af5f046b2d37ef857":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: wound","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.loves-labors-lost.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: wound","exampleIds":[],"headword":"sar","id":"shakespeare014.lex.sore_fourth_year_buck","ipa":"","label":"sore_fourth_year_buck","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"sar","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.loves-labors-lost.r1"}],"definition":"The fourth-year hunting name, rendered as a deliberate poetic homonym of the native wound root for Holofernes's verse.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.sore_fourth_year_buck.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"be87e3dd3811377ced30f3dd6dfa658d9297a07aa8d40bb40c24f4d1a42e6046":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Native lexical derivation from hurt, open","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.venus-and-adonis.r1"}],"etymology":"Native lexical derivation from hurt, open","exampleIds":[],"headword":"murvorh\u00eb\u00c5el","id":"shakespeare014.lex.sore_wound","ipa":"","label":"sore_wound","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"murvorh\u00fc\u0101el","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.venus-and-adonis.r1"}],"definition":"An inflamed or injured bodily place, here the hounds\u2019 wounds.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.sore_wound.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"903ee2936af3be146a49ef1f78ddfec090ec8a2ca536ee4a42cb0c1a0b2d4380":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Native dramatic spelling for the source's sound play.","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.loves-labors-lost.r1"}],"etymology":"Native dramatic spelling for the source's sound play.","exampleIds":[],"headword":"sarl","id":"shakespeare014.lex.sorel_third_year_buck","ipa":"","label":"sorel_third_year_buck","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"sarl","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.loves-labors-lost.r1"}],"definition":"The third-year buck. The written and pronounced l extends the native homonym for sore in the preceding hunting term. This literary spelling lets the addition work onstage.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.sorel_third_year_buck.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"7e1b92c7063b323c5e7c457ed5a057a417f8a0f3df32fd01c476454ab6a4b933":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Reviewed contextual sense/identity of thought","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"etymology":"Reviewed contextual sense/identity of thought","exampleIds":[],"headword":"w\u00c5vumen","id":"shakespeare014.lex.sorrowful_reflection","ipa":"","label":"sorrowful_reflection","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"w\u0101vumen","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"definition":"Thought in Enobarbus\u2019s remorse speech: painful inward reflection which he believes can kill him. Retains its cognitive sense together with the sorrow specified by the scene.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.sorrowful_reflection.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"7b9e38aebf35168716c3e9f7f445cd38d2d15f68147380b700f533195dfa5551":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Author-permitted phonetic adaptation of a personal or place name.","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"etymology":"Author-permitted phonetic adaptation of a personal or place name.","exampleIds":[],"headword":"sosius","id":"shakespeare014.lex.sossius_commander","ipa":"","label":"sossius_commander","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"proper","pronunciati