orn","id":"shakespeare014.lex.wise_old","ipa":"","label":"wise_old","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"h\u0101b\u0117rmemnavorn","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.lucrece.r1"}],"definition":"A person wise with age; contrasts with heedless youth, not a botanical sage. Wise-old is lexicalized as a person noun.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.wise_old.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"68447a8495258497e0eca96be6cd6bd960935b7c97c692ae899009bcad746057":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Reviewed contextual sense of wit","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.shakespeares-sonnets.r1"}],"etymology":"Reviewed contextual sense of wit","exampleIds":[],"headword":"w\u00c5vumjer","id":"shakespeare014.lex.wit_faculty","ipa":"","label":"wit_faculty","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"w\u0101vumjer","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.shakespeares-sonnets.r1"}],"definition":"An inward mental faculty, the source\u2019s five wits in Sonnet 141. Keeps the conventional count without inventing a particular historical list or narrowing wit to verbal cleverness.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.wit_faculty.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"2cb80b3731ee2748ae79de176927f28686ca4c5cf24ea8aaca25f307db93b76d":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Reviewed contextual sense/identity of kjv_witch","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"etymology":"Reviewed contextual sense/identity of kjv_witch","exampleIds":[],"headword":"vekit","id":"shakespeare014.lex.witch_role","ipa":"","label":"witch_role","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"vekit","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"definition":"A witch as the teasing or accusing role assigned by Charmian to the soothsayer. No imported Biblical prohibition or assertion that magic works.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.witch_role.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"c24c959f1be0164c2be78e3bd6285887a83b57bd4405fae953dd9dde2f0bad1b":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation: spell + art","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation: spell + art","exampleIds":[],"headword":"f\u00eblvenjw\u00ebnh\u00c5b\u00cd","id":"shakespeare014.lex.witchcraft_art","ipa":"","label":"witchcraft_art","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"f\u00fclvenjw\u00fcnh\u0101b\u0117r","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.antony-and-cleopatra.r1"}],"definition":"Magical craft in Pompey\u2019s wish that Cleopatra detain Antony. A native spell-art designation, not an ordinary foreign loan or a named religious group.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.witchcraft_art.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"be0c7d291d80607a9bd013a237d2d9214f51dc831cf14b18d20480fcdbc2cf54":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation/sense: enchant + craft","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.coriolanus.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation/sense: enchant + craft","exampleIds":[],"headword":"f\u00eblmirjw\u00ebnh\u00c5b\u00cd","id":"shakespeare014.lex.witchcraft_sorcery","ipa":"","label":"witchcraft_sorcery","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"f\u00fclmirjw\u00fcnh\u0101b\u0117r","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.coriolanus.r1"}],"definition":"Enchanting craft as an image for Coriolanus\u2019s hold on the soldiers. Native enchant-craft; the lieutenant expresses wonder, not proof that literal magic has been performed.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.witchcraft_sorcery.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"be6b6eafe14ca1f1260878744547a6e414cb3a3180a83a36840de12a8158c0e6":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: large + cooking_pot","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.macbeth.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: large + cooking_pot","exampleIds":[],"headword":"gorenborem","id":"shakespeare014.lex.witches_cauldron","ipa":"","label":"witches_cauldron","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"noun","pronunciation":"gorenborem","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.macbeth.r1"}],"definition":"The large pot around which the witches circle and sing.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.witches_cauldron.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"2cea06ad98b6e988a8ff399e79f24e17d4dd2250c79fc5ac62700b9699c2d68f":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Author-permitted geographic-name adaptation","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.hamlet.r1"}],"etymology":"Author-permitted geographic-name adaptation","exampleIds":[],"headword":"vitenberg","id":"shakespeare014.lex.wittenberg_place","ipa":"","label":"wittenberg_place","notes":"","orthographyId":"thimfora-79","partOfSpeech":"proper","pronunciation":"vitenberg","senses":[{"citations":[{"locator":"Authored lexical decision","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.hamlet.r1"}],"definition":"The university city from which Hamlet and Horatio know each other.","id":"shakespeare014.lex.wittenberg_place.sense","status":"draft"}],"status":"draft","variants":[]},"de76c2a73a5582458f3ab9959dc300c8bee98e2b6fe65a063946d201fd76d28e":{"audioLinks":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Authored native derivation or sense: consent + cuckold","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-merry-wives-of-windsor.r1"}],"etymology":"Authored native derivation or sense: consent + cuckold","exampleIds":[],"headword":"\u00c3ahikamo\u00d1kranerzeren","id"