0c7b604d9551a6a311a4930776a02afde4ea1bfd7838a04c76d78d3b2":{"alternatives":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Complete authored source review","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-merchant-of-venice.r1"}],"exampleIds":[],"id":"shakespeare014.review.the-merchant-of-venice.r1","label":"The Merchant of Venice \u00b7 source decisions","morphologyIds":[],"notes":"","statement":"Complete source-linked translation review: 698 original source blocks; 763 layout units.\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 1: 00001a\u201300029a\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 2: 00030a\u201300050a\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 3: 00051a\u201300085a\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 4: 00086a\u201300109a\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 5: 00110a\u201300135a\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 6: 00136a\u201300172a\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 7: 00173a\u201300213a\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 8: 00214a\u201300249a\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 9: 00250a\u201300272b\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 10: 00273a\u201300299a\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 11: 00300a\u201300335a\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 12: 00336a\u201300359b\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 13: 00359c\u201300379a\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 14: 00380a\u201300404a\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 15: 00405a\u201300431a\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 16: 00432a\u201300462b\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 17: 00462c\u201300491a\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 18: 00492a\u201300531a\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 19: 00532a\u201300564a\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 20: 00565a\u201300601a\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 21: 00602a\u201300642a\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 22: 00643a\u201300676a\nWiki the-merchant-of-venice review 23: 00677a\u201300698a","status":"draft","topic":"construction"},"60f22c140068e5806f484496bccfb1c9124148f7d6d9fa942a118d49f6863aa5":{"alternatives":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Complete authored source review","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-merry-wives-of-windsor.r1"}],"exampleIds":[],"id":"shakespeare014.review.the-merry-wives-of-windsor.r1","label":"The Merry Wives of Windsor \u00b7 source decisions","morphologyIds":[],"notes":"","statement":"Complete source-linked translation review: 1126 original source blocks; 1166 layout units.\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 1: 00001a\u201300039a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 2: 00040a\u201300087a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 3: 00088a\u201300133a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 4: 00134a\u201300179a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 5: 00180a\u201300220a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 6: 00221a\u201300265a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 7: 00266a\u201300292a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 8: 00293a\u201300341a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 9: 00342a\u201300378a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 10: 00379a\u201300414a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 11: 00415a\u201300443a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 12: 00444a\u201300489a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 13: 00490a\u201300532a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 14: 00533a\u201300573a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 15: 00574a\u201300620a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 16: 00621a\u201300662a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 17: 00663a\u201300706a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 18: 00707a\u201300747a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 19: 00748a\u201300779a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 20: 00780a\u201300836a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 21: 00837a\u201300882a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 22: 00883a\u201300926a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 23: 00927a\u201300963a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 24: 00964a\u201301008a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 25: 01009a\u201301036a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 26: 01037a\u201301068a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 27: 01069a\u201301106a\nWiki the-merry-wives-of-windsor review 28: 01107a\u201301126a","status":"draft","topic":"construction"},"7223e2d6aa3a9a7417addd2e438ad516bd6f26d8ff3e46aaab54a3e195166278":{"alternatives":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Complete authored source review","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-phoenix-and-turtle"}],"exampleIds":[],"id":"shakespeare014.review.the-phoenix-and-turtle","label":"The Phoenix and Turtle \u00b7 source decisions","morphologyIds":[],"notes":"","statement":"00001 \u00b7 \"The Phoenix and Turtle\"\n\nBack-translation: Phoenix and Turtle [the dove].\n\nReview: Title identities refer to the poem\u2019s birds, not to a reptile or asserted historical people.\n\n00002 \u00b7 Let the bird of loudest lay On the sole Arabian tree Herald sad and trumpet be, To whose sound chaste wings obey.\n\nBack-translation: May the bird of the loudest song, on the sole Arabian tree, be a sorrowful herald and trumpet, whose sound chaste wings obey.\n\nReview: The heralding bird is not arbitrarily identified as the Phoenix. The sole tree and plural chaste wings remain; herald/trumpet retain the ceremonial metaphor.\n\n00003 \u00b7 But thou shrieking harbinger, Foul precurrer of the fiend, Augur of the fever's end, To this troop come thou not near.\n\nBack-translation: But you, shrieking forecomer, foul precursor of the fiend, foreteller of fever\u2019s end: do not come near this gathering.\n\nReview: No unprinted species is assigned to the ominous bird. Fever\u2019s end remains ominous and is not mistranslated as recovery. Troop is a gathering of mourners, not an army.\n\n00004 \u00b7 From this session interdict Every fowl of tyrant wing, Save the eagle, feathered king; Keep the obsequy so strict.\n\nBack-translation: Exclude every tyrant-winged bird from this gathering except the eagle, feathered king; keep the funeral thus strict.\n\nReview: The eagle is the stated exception, not an added prohibition. The imperative\u2019s scope covers the assembly and exact ritual observance.\n\n00005 \u00b7 Let the priest in surplice white, That defunctive music can, Be the death-divining swan, Lest the requiem lack his right.\n\nBack-translation: May the priest in a white surplice, skilled in death-music, be the death-foretelling swan, lest the requiem lack his due.\n\nReview: Can is skill/knowledge, not permission. The possessive his is retained in the due/rite phrase rather than silently deciding every theological ambiguity. Native requiem is a hymn for the dead.\n\n00006 \u00b7 And thou treble-dated crow, That thy sable gender mak'st With the breath thou giv'st and tak'st, 'Mongst our mourners shalt thou go.\n\nBack-translation: And you, three-lived crow, who make your black kind with the breath you give and take, shall go among our mourners.\n\nReview: Treble retains the exact threefold lifetime claim; no comparison species or biological mechanism is invented. Our is plural participants in the mourning gathering. The source\u2019s strange breath-generation assertion is retained.\n\n00007 \u00b7 Here the anthem doth commence: Love and constancy is dead, Phoenix and the turtle fled In a mutual flame from hence.\n\nBack-translation: Here the anthem begins: love and constancy have died; Phoenix and Turtle fled from here in a mutual flame.\n\nReview: The shared flame and death remain. No rebirth is supplied from the usual phoenix myth. The source\u2019s unified predicate is preserved without forcing the birds into first-person pronouns.\n\n00008 \u00b7 So they loved, as love in twain Had the essence but in one, Two distincts, division none; Number there in love was slain.\n\nBack-translation: They loved so that love in two had its essence only in one: two distinct, no division; there number was slain in love.\n\nReview: Preserves the simultaneous distinction and unity, rather than flattening it to ordinary agreement or making two equal zero. Number is cardinal distinction, not a noble Count.\n\n00009 \u00b7 Hearts remote yet not asunder, Distance and no space was seen 'Twixt this turtle and his queen; But in them it were a wonder.\n\nBack-translation: Two hearts remote but not apart; distance and no space were seen between this Turtle and his queen; outside their case it would be a wonder.\n\nReview: The apparently contradictory distance/no-space pairing is kept. But in them is read as except in their exceptional case; this interpretation is explicit, not asserted uniquely certain. His queen preserves the source\u2019s pair relation.\n\n00010 \u00b7 So between them love did shine That the turtle saw his right Flaming in the phoenix' sight; Either was the other's mine.\n\nBack-translation: Love shone between them so that Turtle saw his own right flaming in Phoenix\u2019s sight; each was the other\u2019s own.\n\nReview: Right/claim and reciprocal possession are kept. Mine is read as possessive mine, not a mining excavation; the English source remains available beside the gloss.\n\n00011 \u00b7 Property was thus appalled That the self was not the same; Single nature's double name Neither two nor one was called.\n\nBack-translation: Property was appalled that the self was not the same; the one nature\u2019s twofold name was called neither two nor one.\n\nReview: Property is defining individuality with a possession resonance, not real estate. Both negated alternatives survive. The one/two paradox is deliberately not rationalized.\n\n00012 \u00b7 Reason, in itself confounded, Saw division grow together, To themselves yet either neither, Simple were so well compounded\n\nBack-translation: Reason, confounded within itself, saw division growing together; in relation to themselves each yet neither; the uncombined were so well combined\n\nReview: The logical paradox and initially simple constituents are retained. The sentence continues into the next stanza; a page/stanza boundary does not impose an extra Maxima.\n\n00013 \u00b7 That it cried, \"How true a twain Seemeth this concordant one! Love hath reason, Reason none, If what parts can so remain,\"\n\nBack-translation: that it cried: how truly two this concordant one seems! Love has reason, Reason none, if what separates can remain thus,\n\nReview: Reason\u2019s exclamation is expressed by :excl, not an unsupported exclamation glyph. Lowercase lexical love is used with sentence Maxima. The conditional and Love/Reason reversal remain.\n\n00014 \u00b7 Whereupon it made this threne To the phoenix and the dove, Co-supremes and stars of love, As chorus to their tragic scene.\n\nBack-translation: whereupon it made this mourning-song to Phoenix and Dove, equal supremes and stars of love, as a chorus to their tragic scene.\n\nReview: Dove is the same Turtle protagonist, not a third bird. Co-supremes preserve equal standing. The shift from 13 quatrains to the five-stanza lament remains explicit in the following heading.\n\n00015 \u00b7 Threnos\n\nBack-translation: Mourning-song.\n\nReview: Threnos is translated as a native lament heading, not borrowed as a proper name.\n\n00016 \u00b7 Beauty, truth, and rarity, Grace in all simplicity, Here enclosed, in cinders lie.\n\nBack-translation: Beauty, truth, rarity, grace in all simplicity lie here enclosed in burnt remains.\n\nReview: All five abstract qualities and the burned remains are retained. No claim of physical resurrection is inserted.\n\n00017 \u00b7 Death is now the phoenix' nest, And the turtle's loyal breast To eternity doth rest,\n\nBack-translation: Death is now Phoenix\u2019s nest, and Turtle\u2019s loyal breast rests eternally,\n\nReview: The second-position additive preserves the coordinated second clause without another opening conjunction. The eternal rest continues into the leaving-no-posterity clause.\n\n00018 \u00b7 Leaving no posterity; 'Twas not their infirmity, It was married chastity.\n\nBack-translation: leaving no posterity; it was not their infirmity, it was married chastity.\n\nReview: Infertility is expressly denied; the source\u2019s married chastity is retained rather than explained away. No descendants is not no reputation.\n\n00019 \u00b7 Truth may seem, but cannot be; Beauty brag, but 'tis not she; Truth and beauty buried be.\n\nBack-translation: Truth may seem but cannot be; beauty may boast, but it is not she; truth and beauty are buried.\n\nReview: The contrast between appearance and being, and the feminine Beauty reference, survive. The first modal is possibility; cannot remains inability, not a prohibition. The omitted modal in Beauty brag is recovered from the parallel may-clause, a licensed ellipsis rather than a new tense.\n\n00020 \u00b7 To this urn let those repair That are either true or fair; For these dead birds sigh a prayer.\n\nBack-translation: Let those who are true or fair come to this ashes-vessel; sigh a prayer for these dead birds.\n\nReview: Repair means come, not mend. Either true or fair is preserved as an inclusive invitation. The prayer is for the dead pair, with no added doctrine or rebirth.\n\n00021 \u00b7 William Shakespeare\n\nBack-translation: William Shakespeare.\n\nReview: The supplied author attribution is retained as permitted approximated personal-name sounds.","status":"draft","topic":"construction"},"15c7acda4d153b54c7f45cff7ef186062a0d3b572fcaa0c7243f30a24197b39c":{"alternatives":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Complete authored source review","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-phoenix-and-turtle.r2"}],"exampleIds":[],"id":"shakespeare014.review.the-phoenix-and-turtle.r2","label":"The Phoenix and Turtle \u00b7 source decisions","morphologyIds":[],"notes":"","statement":"00001 \u00b7 \"The Phoenix and Turtle\"\n\nBack-translation: Phoenix and Turtle [the dove].\n\nReview: Title identities refer to the poem\u2019s birds, not to a reptile or asserted historical people.\n\n00002 \u00b7 Let the bird of loudest lay On the sole Arabian tree Herald sad and trumpet be, To whose sound chaste wings obey.\n\nBack-translation: May the bird of the loudest song, on the sole Arabian tree, be a sorrowful herald and trumpet, whose sound chaste wings obey.\n\nReview: The heralding bird is not arbitrarily identified as the Phoenix. The sole tree and plural chaste wings remain; herald/trumpet retain the ceremonial metaphor.\n\n00003 \u00b7 But thou shrieking harbinger, Foul precurrer of the fiend, Augur of the fever's end, To this troop come thou not near.\n\nBack-translation: But you, shrieking forecomer, foul precursor of the fiend, foreteller of fever\u2019s end: do not come near this gathering.\n\nReview: No unprinted species is assigned to the ominous bird. Fever\u2019s end remains ominous and is not mistranslated as recovery. Troop is a gathering of mourners, not an army.\n\n00004 \u00b7 From this session interdict Every fowl of tyrant wing, Save the eagle, feathered king; Keep the obsequy so strict.\n\nBack-translation: Exclude every tyrant-winged bird from this gathering except the eagle, feathered king; keep the funeral thus strict.\n\nReview: The eagle is the stated exception, not an added prohibition. The imperative\u2019s scope covers the assembly and exact ritual observance. Uses the licensed analytic of-phrase to keep the tyrant-wing relation legible without an oversized triple possessive.\n\n00005 \u00b7 Let the priest in surplice white, That defunctive music can, Be the death-divining swan, Lest the requiem lack his right.\n\nBack-translation: May the priest in a white surplice, skilled in death-music, be the death-foretelling swan, lest the requiem lack his due.\n\nReview: Can is skill/knowledge, not permission. The possessive his is retained in the due/rite phrase rather than silently deciding every theological ambiguity. Native requiem is a hymn for the dead.\n\n00006 \u00b7 And thou treble-dated crow, That thy sable gender mak'st With the breath thou giv'st and tak'st, 'Mongst our mourners shalt thou go.\n\nBack-translation: And you, three-lived crow, who make your black kind with the breath you give and take, shall go among our mourners.\n\nReview: Treble retains the exact threefold lifetime claim; no comparison species or biological mechanism is invented. Our is plural participants in the mourning gathering. The source\u2019s strange breath-generation assertion is retained.\n\n00007 \u00b7 Here the anthem doth commence: Love and constancy is dead, Phoenix and the turtle fled In a mutual flame from hence.\n\nBack-translation: Here the anthem begins: love and constancy have died; Phoenix and Turtle fled from here in a mutual flame.\n\nReview: The shared flame and death remain. No rebirth is supplied from the usual phoenix myth. The source\u2019s unified predicate is preserved without forcing the birds into first-person pronouns.\n\n00008 \u00b7 So they loved, as love in twain Had the essence but in one, Two distincts, division none; Number there in love was slain.\n\nBack-translation: They loved so that love in two had its essence only in one: two distinct, no division; there number was slain in love.\n\nReview: Preserves the simultaneous distinction and unity, rather than flattening it to ordinary agreement or making two equal zero. Number is cardinal distinction, not a noble Count.\n\n00009 \u00b7 Hearts remote yet not asunder, Distance and no space was seen 'Twixt this turtle and his queen; But in them it were a wonder.\n\nBack-translation: Two hearts remote but not apart; distance and no space were seen between this Turtle and his queen; outside their case it would be a wonder.\n\nReview: The apparently contradictory distance/no-space pairing is kept. But in them is read as except in their exceptional case; this interpretation is explicit, not asserted uniquely certain. His queen preserves the source\u2019s pair relation.\n\n00010 \u00b7 So between them love did shine That the turtle saw his right Flaming in the phoenix' sight; Either was the other's mine.\n\nBack-translation: Love shone between them so that Turtle saw his own right flaming in Phoenix\u2019s sight; each was the other\u2019s own.\n\nReview: Right/claim and reciprocal possession are kept. Mine is read as possessive mine, not a mining excavation; the English source remains available beside the gloss.\n\n00011 \u00b7 Property was thus appalled That the self was not the same; Single nature's double name Neither two nor one was called.\n\nBack-translation: Property was appalled that the self was not the same; the one nature\u2019s twofold name was called neither two nor one.\n\nReview: Property is defining individuality with a possession resonance, not real estate. Both negated alternatives survive. The one/two paradox is deliberately not rationalized.\n\n00012 \u00b7 Reason, in itself confounded, Saw division grow together, To themselves yet either neither, Simple were so well compounded\n\nBack-translation: Reason, confounded within itself, saw division growing together; in relation to themselves each yet neither; the uncombined were so well combined\n\nReview: The logical paradox and initially simple constituents are retained. The sentence continues into the next stanza; a page/stanza boundary does not impose an extra Maxima.\n\n00013 \u00b7 That it cried, \"How true a twain Seemeth this concordant one! Love hath reason, Reason none, If what parts can so remain,\"\n\nBack-translation: that it cried: how truly two this concordant one seems! Love has reason, Reason none, if what separates can remain thus,\n\nReview: Reason\u2019s exclamation is expressed by :excl, not an unsupported exclamation glyph. Lowercase lexical love is used with sentence Maxima. The conditional and Love/Reason reversal remain.\n\n00014 \u00b7 Whereupon it made this threne To the phoenix and the dove, Co-supremes and stars of love, As chorus to their tragic scene.\n\nBack-translation: whereupon it made this mourning-song to Phoenix and Dove, equal supremes and stars of love, as a chorus to their tragic scene.\n\nReview: Dove is the same Turtle protagonist, not a third bird. Co-supremes preserve equal standing. The shift from 13 quatrains to the five-stanza lament remains explicit in the following heading.\n\n00015 \u00b7 Threnos\n\nBack-translation: Mourning-song.\n\nReview: Threnos is translated as a native lament heading, not borrowed as a proper name.\n\n00016 \u00b7 Beauty, truth, and rarity, Grace in all simplicity, Here enclosed, in cinders lie.\n\nBack-translation: Beauty, truth, rarity, grace in all simplicity lie here enclosed in burnt remains.\n\nReview: All five abstract qualities and the burned remains are retained. No claim of physical resurrection is inserted.\n\n00017 \u00b7 Death is now the phoenix' nest, And the turtle's loyal breast To eternity doth rest,\n\nBack-translation: Death is now Phoenix\u2019s nest, and Turtle\u2019s loyal breast rests eternally,\n\nReview: The second-position additive preserves the coordinated second clause without another opening conjunction. The eternal rest continues into the leaving-no-posterity clause.\n\n00018 \u00b7 Leaving no posterity; 'Twas not their infirmity, It was married chastity.\n\nBack-translation: leaving no posterity; it was not their infirmity, it was married chastity.\n\nReview: Infertility is expressly denied; the source\u2019s married chastity is retained rather than explained away. No descendants is not no reputation.\n\n00019 \u00b7 Truth may seem, but cannot be; Beauty brag, but 'tis not she; Truth and beauty buried be.\n\nBack-translation: Truth may seem but cannot be; beauty may boast, but it is not she; truth and beauty are buried.\n\nReview: The contrast between appearance and being, and the feminine Beauty reference, survive. The first modal is possibility; cannot remains inability, not a prohibition. The omitted modal in Beauty brag is recovered from the parallel may-clause, a licensed ellipsis rather than a new tense.\n\n00020 \u00b7 To this urn let those repair That are either true or fair; For these dead birds sigh a prayer.\n\nBack-translation: Let those who are true or fair come to this ashes-vessel; sigh a prayer for these dead birds.\n\nReview: Repair means come, not mend. Either true or fair is preserved as an inclusive invitation. The prayer is for the dead pair, with no added doctrine or rebirth.\n\n00021 \u00b7 William Shakespeare\n\nBack-translation: William Shakespeare.\n\nReview: The supplied author attribution is retained as permitted approximated personal-name sounds.","status":"draft","topic":"construction"},"7e3a74bafa52e82fb95855fe898152f04b42e3ac7baba6ce5a47dbb2c5916a28":{"alternatives":[],"citations":[{"locator":"Complete authored source review","sourceId":"shakespeare014.source.the-taming-of-the-shrew.r1"}],"exampleIds":[],"id":"shakespeare014.review.the-taming-of-the-shrew.r1","label":"The Taming of the Shrew \u00b7 source decisions","morphologyIds":[],"notes":"","statement":"Complete source-linked translation review: 983 original source blocks; 1023 layout units.\nWiki the-taming-of-the-shrew review 1: 00001a\u201300032a\nWiki the-taming-of-the-shrew review 2: 00033a\u201300053a\nWiki the-taming-of-the-shrew review 3: 00054a\u201300087b\nWiki the-taming-of-the-shrew review 4: 00088a\u201300121a\nWiki the-taming-of-the-shrew review 5: 00122a\u201300157a\nWiki the-taming-of-the-shrew review 6: 00158a\u201300184a\nWiki the-taming-of-the-shrew review 7: 00185a\u201300229a\nWiki the-taming-of-the-shrew review 8: 00230a\u201300267a\nWiki the-taming-of-the-shrew review 9: 00268a\u201300298a\nWiki the-taming-of-the-shrew review 10: 00299a\u201300348a\nWiki the-taming-of-the-shrew review 11: 00348b\u201300382a\nWiki the-taming-of-the-shrew review 12: 00382b\u201300408a\nWiki the-taming-of-the-shrew review 13: 00409a\u201300445a\nWiki the-taming-of-the-shrew review 14: 00445b\u201300483a\nWiki the-taming-of-the-shrew review 15: 00484a\u201300521a\nWiki the-taming-of-the-shrew review 16: 00522a\u201300567a\nWiki the-taming-of-the-shrew review 17: 00568a\u201300603a\nWiki the-taming-of-the-shrew review 18: 00604a\u201300645a\nWiki the-t